# ADR-0005: Canon Governance Patterns Adopted from the Biases as Constants Manuscript

## Status

Proposed — pending Jamie's confirmation, following the same posture as ADR-0004: the pattern selection is made here, but scope, exact fields, and timing need sign-off before implementation starts.

## Context

Source material: `vault-codices-biases-as-constants-v10.1.0.docx` (the actual BAC book manuscript, ~29,300 words), staged from `C:\Users\jamie\OKH-Local\04_GitHub_Mirrors\vault-codices-biases-as-constants\manuscript\` on 2026-08-18 and converted to text via `pandoc`. This is a different source than the ChatGPT thread captured in `docs/thread-extracts/mps-bac-thread-naming-and-platform-split.md` on the same day.

That distinction matters: this session ran a full-text search for the platform/product-surface terms the ChatGPT thread's Notion-update summary named — Canon Rings, Echo Vault, Ghost Relay, Narrative Selection Engine, consent boundary, monetization ladder, the three-surface platform split, "GitHub holds / Replit executes" — and **none of them appear anywhere in this manuscript.** Those apparently live only in Notion PRDs (Vault Reader PRD, Canon Collaboration Platform PRD, Vault Codices Platform Split) that this session still has no access to. Open questions 15.8–15.11 in `docs/decisions/open-questions.md` remain exactly as open as before this ADR; this document does not touch them.

What this manuscript actually contains, instead, is BAC's canon-governance philosophy and mechanics, written in-universe as "the Codex": the PME/PIE/CME/CIE rung ladder (R0–R10) that Telling Forward (TF) already ported per `telling_forward_ui_vision.md` — not revisited here — plus four governance patterns TF has not yet considered:

1. A **Traceability Matrix**: a per-term/motif table (Origin Chapter, Mutation Log, Last Verified In, Canon Status) tracking how a named canon element changed across the saga.
2. A **Terminology Incursion Ledger (TIL)**: tracks canonical-term usage for overuse/saturation ("recursion fatigue") across the whole work, so a term's meaning doesn't erode from overexposure.
3. **Cluster Sovereignty / the Law of Locality**: content is organized into sovereign scopes ("clusters"); a scope's internal rules don't apply outside it, and cross-scope influence is forbidden unless explicitly declared as an override. Enforced by a stated rule: *"No Codex process may invoke or fallback into a sibling cluster without an override declared in the Execution Contract. Violation of cluster locality constitutes symbolic contamination."*
4. A **Compliance Checklist**: a structural pre-flight gate (declared scope, valid ID, fallback defined, glossary-safe, traceable, auditable, purpose stated) a process must pass before it's allowed into the system.

The manuscript also names the governing philosophy directly, useful as citeable grounding for a decision TF already made: *"Canon is earned through execution, not assigned through reputation. ... A clause that cannot withstand contradiction will not fossilize."* This is the same "Canon as Code" framing that later surfaces as a page title in the Notion hub (per the ChatGPT thread extract) — this manuscript is its original articulation.

## Decision

Adopt three of the four patterns above, scoped down to TF's existing "stay simple" instinct — the same instinct that kept the submission-state model at four/six states instead of porting BAC's seven-state canon vocabulary.

**1. Term/Motif Ledger.** Every named canon entity (character, place, invented term) gets a lightweight, derived record: where it originated (first capsule or scene), its current canon status, and a change log as branches get accepted into canon. This is read-only and generated from existing capsule/scene metadata — not a new authoring surface, and not the manuscript's full symbolic-load/saturation scoring. Purpose: give a world-creator visibility into term drift across many independent contributors, which a single-author saga doesn't need but a multi-author open-canon platform does.

**2. Branch Locality Rule.** A contributor's story path is a sovereign scope. Canon or world-rule changes made within one story path are not canon-wide until the world-creator explicitly accepts them into canon. TF's branch/canon model already implies this, but no document states it as an explicit rule with a named failure mode. Adopt the manuscript's framing directly: undeclared cross-branch influence is contamination, not a shortcut.

**3. Promotion Compliance Checklist**, TF-scoped. A short structural checklist a capsule/scene passes before "Promote to scene" or "Submit your scene": does it declare which storyworld/story-path it belongs to, does it reference existing canon terms consistently with the Term/Motif Ledger, is there a stated purpose for the change. Human-reviewed at first, consistent with ADR-0002's boundary that agents triage but don't make the final creative call. This is a TF-appropriate subset of the manuscript's ten-criteria checklist, not that checklist verbatim — those ten criteria are tuned to a single-author recursive-prose engine's failure modes (rung dependence, echo torque, blackbox behavior), most of which don't map to a multi-author story platform.

**Explicitly not adopted:**

- The four-tier symbolic execution hierarchy with automatic upward failure-escalation (Cluster → Process → Procedure → State). Real pattern, real value for a complex automation pipeline, but more machinery than TF's current three-tier ingestion design (ADR-0004) or two-action Concept Board (Promote; Disrupt/Invert) need today. Worth revisiting only if TF's own automation grows enough tiers to need it.
- Full symbolic-load/cadence scoring (recursion fatigue thresholds, echo torque, tier-locked reader gating). This is BAC's narrative-craft tooling for one author's prose rhythm across a single saga, not a multi-author collaboration-governance concern. Out of scope for TF.

## Consequences

- The Term/Motif Ledger can only be built once capsules/scenes carry structured references to named entities. Per `telling_forward_ui_vision.md`, no `capsules` table exists in the repository yet. This ADR is a design commitment for when that table is designed, not something to implement standalone this week — the schema for capsules should account for a term/entity reference field from the start rather than bolting one on later.
- The Branch Locality Rule is a documentation change more than a code change: add explicit language to `docs/MISSION.md` or `CONTRIBUTING.md` stating that story-path-local changes aren't canon until accepted, and naming the failure mode (silent cross-branch canon contamination) it prevents. Low cost, can happen independent of the other two items.
- The Promotion Compliance Checklist needs Jamie to define TF's actual minimal criteria before it's built — this ADR adopts the pattern, not a specific list.
- This ADR does not resolve open-questions 15.8–15.11; those stay blocked on material this manuscript doesn't contain.
- Adds one new open question, 15.12 (recorded in `docs/decisions/open-questions.md`): what specific fields belong in the Term/Motif Ledger, and does it get designed into the `capsules` table's schema before or alongside the table's first implementation.

## Gaps this ADR closes

Gives Telling Forward a citeable intellectual grounding — "canon is earned through execution/review, not assigned through reputation" — for the structured-canon-state design it already committed to (capsules, GitHub Issues, the four/six-state review model), useful when explaining that design to a new contributor or collaborator who asks why canon isn't just "whatever the world-creator says."

Does not close the four-vs-six submission-state discrepancy (`docs/adr/0004-manuscript-ingestion-and-bring-your-own-ai.md`, Discrepancy 1) or open-questions 15.8–15.11 — none of the concepts those reference (Canon Rings, Echo Vault, Ghost Relay, Narrative Selection Engine, consent boundary, the third platform surface) appear anywhere in this manuscript. If those terms are defined in the Notion PRDs, that's a separate follow-up: paste the relevant Notion page content, or connect a Notion source, and this session can extract them the same way.
